    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "No worries, no worries. I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's get back to the engineering part.\n\nSo, when the turbine spins, it drives the compressor to suck in more air, which increases the pressure in the compressor discharge. This increased pressure is then transmitted to the turbine side through the shaft.\n\nAs the turbine spins, it accelerates the exhaust gases, which increases the pressure in the exhaust manifold. This increased pressure is then transferred to the compressor side through the turbine shaft, causing the compressor to suck in more air.\n\nThe pressure increase in the manifold is due to the conservation of energy principle. The turbine extracts energy from the exhaust gases, which is then transferred to the compressor, causing it to compress the air. This compression increases the pressure in the manifold, which drives the compressor to suck in more air.\n\nThink of it like a seesaw: the turbine extracts energy from the exhaust gases, and the compressor uses that energy to compress the air, increasing the pressure in the manifold. The pressure increase in the manifold is what drives the compressor to suck in more air, creating a positive feedback loop.\n\nNow, to answer your question directly, the pressure increase in the manifold is typically measured in terms of absolute pressure",
